alex: Quick one — during a customer pairing we noticed our SDK types go from `unknown` to `any` after the ESM edge publish. Seems specific to consumers using moduleResolution=bundler. Minimal repro attached.

priya: can you paste the tiny repro?

alex: Yep. SDK API:
```ts
// sdk/src/transport.ts
export function dispatch<T>(msg: T): Promise<{ ok: boolean; payload: T }>;
export function dispatch(msg: unknown) { return Promise.resolve({ ok: true, payload: msg as any }); }
```
consumer:
```ts
import { dispatch } from '@redwood/sdk';

type Q = { name: string };
async function go(){
  const r = await dispatch<Q>({ name: 'a' });
  // Expected: r.payload -> Q. Observed: r.payload is `any` when imported via ESM entry after app bundling for Edge.
}
```

kevin: does the CJS package keep the right types?

alex: yes — cjs/index.d.ts preserves generics and overloads. The published ESM entry (dist/esm/index.d.ts) appears to have been generated by our dts bundler and shows a single implementation signature with widened types. The difference only shows up when consumer tsconfig uses `moduleResolution: "bundler"` (typical for edge apps).

priya: suspicious: if `exports.import` points at a file whose companion .d.ts was produced by the bundler and overwritten, TS will pick that up. Also double-check `types`/`typesVersions`. TS resolution rules are picky with bundler mode.

alex: Exactly. In our case `package.json` has: 
```json
{"exports": {"import": "dist/esm/index.js","require": "dist/cjs/index.js"}, "types": "dist/types/index.d.ts"}
```
But the published tarball contains `dist/esm/index.d.ts` produced by rollup-plugin-dts that flattened overloads, and because consumers resolve to the `import` path the bundler d.ts wins.

kevin: short-term workaround for customers: deep-import the CJS types: `import { dispatch } from '@redwood/sdk/dist/cjs'` or add a types-only import: `import type { dispatch } from '@redwood/sdk/dist/types'`. Ugly but a stopgap.

priya: long-term fixes: 1) ensure `exports.import` points to the compiled esm file whose d.ts we control (avoid overwriting with a generated bundle), or 2) switch d.ts generator to api-extractor with a config that preserves overloads, or 3) keep a hand-maintained top-level index.d.ts that declares public overloads explicitly.

kevin: plus add a CI smoke: pack the artifact, install into a tiny edge fixture with `moduleResolution: bundler`, and run `npx tsc --noEmit`. Also emit declarations for both cjs and esm and run a structural diff (use `api-extractor` or `ts-api-guardian`). Fail the build on shrinkage.

types-bot: Created ticket RW-DX-9099: "ESM d.ts bundling flattens overloads and widens unknown -> any for Edge consumers" (triaged).

alex: i'll attach the repro and a quick script that diffs the two index.d.ts files in the tarball. Also adding a tiny vercel fixture to our mono repo for presubmit checks.

priya: I'll update the SDK README with a short troubleshooting note pointing to the deep-import workaround and the tsconfig flags to watch (moduleResolution, types, typesVersions). Also add a link to the ticket.

kevin: i'll pick up the packaging change. Plan: 1) stop shipping rollup-plugin-dts output as the primary types entry, 2) generate a curated index.d.ts via api-extractor if bundling is necessary, 3) add the CI check alex suggested. Will post a PR.

alex: thanks — appreciate the quick triage. :thumbsup:
